──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Q26. What is the difference between SQL and NoSQL? Why did you choose NoSQL?
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANSWER:
  "SQL databases (like MySQL, PostgreSQL) store data in structured tables with
   fixed schemas. NoSQL databases (like Firestore, MongoDB) store data as
   documents or key-value pairs with flexible schemas. I chose Firestore
   (NoSQL) because resume data is hierarchical — a resume contains arrays
   of education, experience, projects — which maps naturally to a document
   model. Also, Firestore scales automatically and offers real-time
   synchronization, which would be complex to implement with SQL."

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Q27. What is CORS? Did you face it?
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANSWER:
  "CORS stands for Cross-Origin Resource Sharing. It's a browser security
   mechanism that prevents a web page from making requests to a different
   domain unless that domain explicitly allows it. Since I was calling the
   Google Gemini API directly from the browser (client-side), and Google
   configures their own CORS headers to allow browser-side API calls,
   I did not face CORS issues. However, if I had built a custom backend
   API, I would need to configure CORS headers."

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Q28. What is lazy loading / code splitting? Did you implement it?
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANSWER:
  "Lazy loading means loading parts of the application only when they are
   needed, rather than loading everything at once. Vite supports automatic
   code splitting — it splits the bundle into chunks for each route/view.
   In my app, each view like Editor, Dashboard, ATSScoreChecker etc. is a
   separate component that can be lazy loaded using React.lazy() if needed.
   This reduces initial load time significantly."

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Q29. What is a custom hook in React? Why did you create useAuth?
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANSWER:
  "A custom hook in React is a function that starts with 'use' and contains
   reusable logic using other React hooks. I created useAuth to encapsulate
   Firebase's authentication state listener — onAuthStateChanged. Instead
   of repeating that subscription logic in every component that needs to
   know if a user is logged in, I put it in useAuth which returns the
   current user and loading state. Any component can then call useAuth()
   and get that data cleanly."

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Q30. How does Google Gemini AI generate text? What is a prompt?
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANSWER:
  "Google Gemini is a Large Language Model (LLM) — an AI trained on massive
   amounts of text data that can understand and generate human-like text.
   A prompt is the instruction or question you send to the AI. In my project,
   I craft detailed system prompts — for example, for generating a resume
   summary, I tell the AI the candidate's details, the target job role, and
   strict rules like 'do not use first-person language' and 'do not invent
   facts'. The AI processes this and returns a well-crafted summary.
   The quality of output depends heavily on how well the prompt is written
   — this is called Prompt Engineering."

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Q31. What is localStorage? How did you use it?
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANSWER:
  "localStorage is a browser API that allows storing key-value pairs
   persistently in the browser — the data survives page refreshes and
   even browser restarts. I used localStorage to cache AI API responses.
   When Gemini returns a result, I store it with a hash key in localStorage.
   If the same prompt is made again, I serve the cached result without
   making an API call. I also implement LRU eviction — when the cache hits
   50 entries, I remove the oldest one to prevent localStorage from
   filling up."

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Q32. What is Tailwind CSS? How is it different from regular CSS?
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANSWER:
  "Tailwind CSS is a utility-first CSS framework. Instead of writing custom
   CSS classes, you apply pre-built utility classes directly in your HTML/JSX.
   For example, instead of writing .button { background: blue; padding: 8px },
   you write className='bg-blue-500 p-2'. This speeds up development and
   avoids writing separate CSS files. Tailwind also has tree-shaking built
   in — unused styles are removed from the production build, keeping the
   CSS bundle very small."

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Q33. What is Framer Motion? Why did you use it?
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANSWER:
  "Framer Motion is a React animation library. It makes it very easy to add
   smooth animations and transitions to React components using a declarative
   syntax. I used it to add entrance animations, hover effects, and smooth
   transitions between views in the app — for example, animating the resume
   cards on the dashboard. It works well with React because animations are
   tied to component lifecycle events."

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Q34. What is an API key? Is it secure to use it in the frontend?
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANSWER:
  "An API key is a unique identifier used to authenticate requests to an API.
   Using API keys directly in the frontend (browser) is generally not ideal
   because the key can be found by inspecting network requests. However,
   Google's Gemini API and Firebase are designed to be used from the browser —
   Firebase has its own security rules system (Firestore rules) to control
   data access, and Gemini keys can have domain restrictions. I mitigated
   the risk further by never committing keys to GitHub, using .env files
   locally, and configuring environment variables in Vercel/Netlify."

